BEGIN
UPDATE c SET Meaning = N'Khá, mãi không', Formation = N'なかなか ＋ vị ngữ khẳng định hoặc phủ định', Explanation = N'Với khẳng định, chỉ mức độ khá cao. Với phủ định, nhấn mạnh việc mãi chưa xảy ra hoặc khó thực hiện.', UsageNotes = NULL, UpdatedAt = SYSUTCDATETIME()
FROM Grammars c WHERE c.Id = @Id AND c.Meaning COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 = N'quite; considerably; (with a negative) just won''t ~' AND DATALENGTH(c.Meaning) = DATALENGTH(N'quite; considerably; (with a negative) just won''t ~') AND c.Formation COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 = N'なかなか ＋ ［affirmative predicate］
なかなか ＋ Verb［ない form］' AND DATALENGTH(c.Formation) = DATALENGTH(N'なかなか ＋ ［affirmative predicate］
なかなか ＋ Verb［ない form］') AND c.Explanation COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 = N'なかなか has two everyday jobs. Before an affirmative word it means ''quite / considerably'', often with a touch of surprise that something beats your expectations — なかなかおいしい = surprisingly tasty. Before a negative verb (なかなか〜ない) it means the action is slow or hard to happen — ''just won''t ~'' — as in バスがなかなか来ない (''the bus just won''t come''). Read the polarity of the sentence to tell the two uses apart.' AND DATALENGTH(c.Explanation) = DATALENGTH(N'なかなか has two everyday jobs. Before an affirmative word it means ''quite / considerably'', often with a touch of surprise that something beats your expectations — なかなかおいしい = surprisingly tasty. Before a negative verb (なかなか〜ない) it means the action is slow or hard to happen — ''just won''t ~'' — as in バスがなかなか来ない (''the bus just won''t come''). Read the polarity of the sentence to tell the two uses apart.') AND c.UsageNotes COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 = N'With a negative, なかなか〜ない means something is slow or hard to happen (''just won''t ~''), not merely ''not very''.
In the affirmative ''quite'' sense it carries mild admiration or surprise that something exceeds what you expected, so it suits praise more than plain measurement.' AND DATALENGTH(c.UsageNotes) = DATALENGTH(N'With a negative, なかなか〜ない means something is slow or hard to happen (''just won''t ~''), not merely ''not very''.
In the affirmative ''quite'' sense it carries mild admiration or surprise that something exceeds what you expected, so it suits praise more than plain measurement.');
